Patch 0086: the IOP scheduler picks among equal-priority ready threads in the console's order (FIFO by the moment each became Ready).
What: IopThread gets readyOrder, set from an increasing counter by IopKernel::makeReady() every time a thread enters Ready
from a non-running state (StartThread, WakeupThread/iWakeup, ResumeThread, ReleaseWaitThread, semaphore signal, event flag
set/wait satisfied, delay expiry in beginNextReady) and when a running thread yields (YieldThread/RotateThreadReadyQueue:
back of its queue). A thread preempted while running (slice end) keeps its readyOrder, i.e. its place at the head.
beginNextReady picks the lowest priority number, then the lowest readyOrder (the tie break by lowest thread id is gone); the
CpuSuspendIntr held-thread path and the maxPriority filter are unchanged.
Why: with the id tie break, cri_adxi's tick thread 8 (prio 39), woken by a delay, preempted thread 10 (prio 39) between its
left and right PCM puts; the per-channel freed list sent to the EE came up one 0x400 block short on R and the opening movie's
sound doubled. The console's thbase only preempts for a strictly higher priority.
Verified: ps2xIOP/tests/iop_import_tests.cpp testReadyOrder (equal-priority woken thread does not preempt the running one;
a higher priority one does; after it sleeps the preempted thread resumes before the later-woken one) passes ("ps2xIOP import
tests passed"); tests/regression/test_movie_audio.py -m slow passes (failed before).

